    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The epic grew from five Sumerian poems about <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilgamesh">Gilgamesh</a>, king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruk">Uruk</a>, into an Akkadian epic whose Old Babylonian version dates to the 18th century BCE and whose Standard Babylonian version was compiled by Sîn-lēqi-unninni.</li><li>It tells of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilgamesh">Gilgamesh</a>'s friendship with the wild man Enkidu, their killing of Humbaba and the Bull of Heaven, Enkidu's death, and Gilgamesh's failed search for eternal life, which brings him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ziusudra">Utnapishtim</a>, survivor of the flood.</li><li>Recovered from the library of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashurbanipal">Ashurbanipal</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nineveh">Nineveh</a> in the 1850s, it has been compared with the Genesis flood narrative, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a> and with the Alexander legends, and new fragments are still being joined to it.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a> signs of his name could be pronounced accurately, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilgamesh">Gilgamesh</a> was first reintroduced to the modern world as “Izdubar”.</p></aside>
    </section>

